    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Published at a time when the Los Angeles Riots remained fresh in America’s mind, Chang-Rae Lee’s novel, Native Speaker, skyrocketed to critical acclaim. Kim’s paper explores how Native Speaker navigates the implications of belonging and control over a narrative. This research focuses on how the narration of a traumatic moment can become an entry point for understanding the narrator’s agency, and what such readings might illuminate in transnational literatures.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
